          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Decision relevance vs. analytical interestingness — a clear contrast",
          "lede": "Two analysts look at the same QuickShip data and choose different findings to lead with. Only one is using the right criterion.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_prioritizing_framing_context.r01.b1",
              "role": "example",
              "text": "Analyst A leads with Kano's high delivery-time variability — the most statistically unusual finding. Analyst B leads with the Lagos slowdown — the finding most directly tied to the staffing decision. The Head of Operations is deciding whether to add Lagos couriers. Analyst B is correct: the criterion is relevance to the decision, not analytical novelty.",
              "lineage": {
