          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.actionable_recommendation_framing"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Finding vs. recommendation — the structural difference",
          "lede": "A finding describes what the data shows. A recommendation states what action to take and what the reader is being asked to do.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_prioritizing_framing_context.r02.b1",
              "role": "example",
              "text": "Finding: \"Lagos delivery times rose to 30 minutes — above the 25-minute target.\" This describes the data. It does not tell the Head of Operations what to do. Recommendation: \"We recommend adding one Lagos courier; please approve the added headcount.\" This names the action and the explicit ask. A recommendation is not a louder restatement of the finding — it is a distinct statement of what should happen next.",
              "lineage": {
